**Supplementary Table 2. Significant associations between measures of symptom severity and deviations from the normative ranges.** The individuals with schizophrenia were subdivided into two mutually exclusive groups for each gray and white matter region investigated. One group comprised individuals with an infra-normal deviation (Group 1), while all remaining individuals (i.e. supra-normal or within the normative range) were allocated to a second group (Group 2). Regions for which Group 1 comprised fewer than 20 individuals were excluded to ensure adequate statistical power. The null hypothesis of equality in DIP scores between Group 1 and 2 was tested independently for each DIP item and region using a two-sample t-test, where p-values were estimated using permutation testing (5000 permutations). The false discovery rate (FDR) was controlled at 5% across the set of all regions and DIP items. The null hypothesis was rejected for three DIP items, as shown in the table. The table shows the mean and standard deviation for the DIP items for each of the two groups. L: left. R: right. DIP: Diagnostic Interview for Psychoses. *Intrusive voices talking to the respondent in an accusatory, abusive, persecutory or commanding manner.

| Associations            | Associations               | *Group 1*  Infra-Normal   |      | *Group 2*  Supra-Normal &amp; Normal   |      | *P*  -value   |
|-------------------------|----------------------------|---------------------------|------|----------------------------------------|------|---------------|
| Region                  | DIP Measurement            | Mean                      | Std. | Mean                                   | Std. |               |
| R middle temporal       | Depression: Loss of Energy | 1.2                       | 1.4  | 2.3                                    | 1.2  | 2×10-4        |
| L Lateral orbitofrontal | Hallucinations: Abusive*   | 0.5                       | 0.9  | 1.2                                    | 0.9  | 2×10-4        |
| L Caudal middle frontal | Delusions: Grandiose       | 1.2                       | 1.0  | 0.7                                    | 0.7  | 4×10-4        |

![Image](./41380_2020_882_MOESM1_ESM_artifacts/image_000000_5e29c09ab674d6146d4174b8238feca58c5849d251babbed9715f9b800fd27f8.png)

Supplementary Figure 1. Schematic diagram of methodology. (a) Quantile regression was used to establish the normative range of individual variation in brain structure for a given age and sex. Percentile curves (5%, 50%, 95%) are shown as a function of age for cortical thickness (CT) in two example cortical regions (upper) and fractional anisotropy (FA) in two white matter tracts (lower). Each solid circle denotes a healthy comparison individual. Shading denotes 95% confidence intervals. Cortical regions are shown rendered onto the cortical surface, while regions delineating white matter tracts are shown atop of the reference brain image. (b) For each cortical region and white matter tract, individuals with schizophrenia were compared to the normative range established for their age and sex. For a given region or tract, individuals significantly exceeding the 95% percentile were classified as supra-normal (red boxes; i.e. higher CT or FA than expected) and those significantly below the 5% percentile were classified as infra-normal (blue boxes; i.e. lower CT or FA than expected). All remaining individuals were deemed to reside within the normative range (black boxes) for their age and sex. Note that an individual could reside within the normative range for one region, but be supra- or infra-normal for others. The vector shown provides a putative neural fingerprint that summarizes this information across all regions and tracts. For illustrative purposes, the numbers of elements (boxes) comprising the vector shown is fewer than the actual number of regions and tracts analyzed. (c-e) The matrix in (d) shows fingerprints for several individuals (rows: regions/tracts, columns: individuals). Encoding each region and tract with either +1 (supra-normal, red boxes), 0 (normal, black) or  $-$ 1 (infra-normal, blue) and computing the average of the vector yielded a summary measure of deviation for each individual, called the average abnormality index (AAI). Correlation analysis was used to test whether individual variation in the AAI associated with polygenic risk for schizophrenia (c), and measures of cognition and symptoms (e).

